re related to fertility preservation by discussing, reviewing, planning preservation options, and coordinating next steps with fertility/reproductive specialists as needed. This indicates an orchestrating role across services rather than a single point intervention. [Data: Entities (1487); Relationships (2412)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Reproductive endocrinologists are explicitly tied to fertility preservation services\",\"explanation\":\"A reproductive endocrinologist is defined as a physician specializing in fertility and reproductive hormone-related conditions, and is commonly consulted when a person wants to preserve fertility (including egg or embryo freezing) or when fertility-sparing treatments are being planned. This frames the reproductive endocrinologist as a specialized provider for preservation-oriented care decisions. [Data: Entities (4677); Relationships (10117)]\\n\\nThe dataset directly connects reproductive endocrinologists to fertility preservation by advising that when fertility preservation is desired, the patient should see a reproductive endocrinologist specializing in fertility options. This suggests a clear, recommended pathway: preservation intent → specialist consultation. [Data: Entities (4677); Relationships (10117)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Fertility counseling includes pregnancy-prevention considerations during treatment\",\"explanation\":\"Fertility counseling is described as an as-needed counseling service during cancer care planning to help individuals understand and prepare for potential treatment effects on fertility, including discussions of reproductive goals and available fertility-preservation or family-building options. It is also specifically described as relevant prior to initiating certain therapies (e.g., endocrine therapy) and may involve referral to a fertility specialist when future childbearing is desired. [Data: Entities (1455, 1487); Relationships (11683)]\\n\\nThe community also links fertility counseling with preventing pregnancy during treatment as part of broader health/fertility considerations. This indicates counseling can cover both future-oriented fertility preservation and near-term risk management (pregnancy prevention while on therapy). [Data: Entities (1455); Relationships (9619)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Nutrition support (dietitians/nutritionists) is integrated via referrals and meal-planning support\",\"explanation\":\"The registered dietitian and nutritionist appear as supportive-care entities that can be engaged through referral to provide dietary guidance, meal planning, and nutrition support during treatment and recovery. This establishes nutrition services as part of coordinated cancer care rather than an optional, disconnected resource. [Data: Entities (2100, 2101, 2123); Relationships (3567, 3569)]\\n\\nMeal planning is explicitly connected to registered dietitians, with the dataset stating that a registered dietitian may help plan healthy meals to improve nutrition and support treatment outcomes. This reinforces the operational function of nutrition services in the care pathway. [Data: Entities (2141, 2100); Relationships (3568)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Second-opinion seeking is a structured process that relies on recommendations/referrals\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes a second-opinion pathway: “getting a second opinion” involves consulting a second-opinion doctor, and the second-opinion doctor is tied to referral/recommendation as the mechanism for being directed to another clinician. This depicts second-opinion care as a formalized step supported by the care system rather than an informal activity. [Data: Entities (6384, 6398, 2123); Relationships (14628, 14638)]\\n\\nTaken together with the broader definition of referral and the Cancer Care Team’s coordinating role, the dataset implies that obtaining additional evaluation is part of the same coordination ecosystem used for fertility and nutrition services—i.e., specialized support is accessed through structured hand